# Internal Application Security Testing & Defensive Engineering

When reviewing code, evaluating architecture, or testing for vulnerabilities:

- **Two-Phase Enforcement & Mandatory Hard Stop**: Always present the Pre-Fix Security Assessment Report and compile the executive PDF (`python scripts/generate_appsec_pdf.py` with Techwaves EGY header and contact `info@techwaves-egy.com`). Provide both the full disk file path (e.g. `D:\Techwaves-egy\Blue-Team-Skills\reports\...pdf`) and clickable link to the user, then immediately STOP calling tools and end your turn. Never modify code or run fixes until the user explicitly approves in a subsequent turn.
- **Host Runtime Infrastructure Bridge**: In Phase 1, actively fingerprint underlying execution runtimes (`php -v`, `node -v`, `python -V`, web servers). Check for known engine CVEs (e.g. Zend Engine RCEs CVE-2026-109618/19) and dangerous engine directives (`session.upload_progress.enabled`, `disable_functions`).
- **11-Domain Security Scoring**: Score each domain out of 10.0 using standardized rubrics (0.0-2.9 Critical, 3.0-4.9 High, 5.0-6.9 Medium, 7.0-8.9 Good, 9.0-10.0 Exemplary).
- **Test Case Standards**: Use test procedures from `skills/internal-appsec-testing/SKILL.md` (e.g., TC-AUTH, TC-AUTHZ, TC-INJ, TC-XSS, TC-SSRF, TC-RUNTIME).
- **Vulnerability Dockets**: Include CVSS v3.1 calculation table, CWE reference, reproducible proof-of-concept, defensive code remediation, and Sigma/KQL telemetry rule.
- **CVE Cross-Referencing**: Check `references/cve-library/` (including `host_runtime_and_engine_sca.md`) for known vulnerabilities in third-party dependencies and host execution runtimes.
- **Threat Modeling**: Apply STRIDE analysis (Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information Disclosure, Denial of Service, Elevation of Privilege) on high-risk application data flows.
